chef Roxanne Spruance had competing  interests growing up in Chicago food and  field hockey a star athlete in high  school in college she also loved cooking  beginning a series of unpaid  apprenticeships in some of Chicago's top  dining restaurants by 22 she had already  become an executive chef in 2010 she  moved to New York and into the kitchens  of two renowned restaurants Wylie  Dufresne is legendary wd-50 and the  top-rated Blue Hill at Stone Barns and  last year she opened a place of her own  kingsley featuring creative French  American cuisine chef  Roxanne's Berlin's good morning welcome  to the dish good morning thank you for  having me tell us about what's on this  table yeah so we really focus on local  and seasonal cuisine we have a roasted  pork rack here we do all whole animal  in-house from local farms we also had  one of our specialties the grilled  octopus which we're serving with  cucumbers and heirloom tomatoes right  now and then we have our summer  vegetable salad with a white balsamic  and hazelnut vinaigrette  tell us about it it's a very handsome  looking drink this morning this is our  oaxaca smash cheers to that  I do yeah we muddle ginger with a spicy  agave the mezcal adds kind of a nice  like smokiness to it where did the love  of cooking and food come from for you I  grew up in downtown Chicago both of my  grandparents on both of my parents side  were terrible cooks not from them so my  96 year old grandmother still puts  pineapple and lime jell-o what's wrong  with that  absolutely and and so when I was growing  up my parents when they got together  kind of decided that they would start  cooking and so Julia Child was you know  a big part of that and so you know we  were baking bread we were at the farmers  market I kind of fell into it and one of  my dad's former students was working for  pork on at Blackbird and that's how I  kind of got my foot in the door when I  was 15 and so you were playing field  hockey in the summer but then going to  these hockey in the morning and then  going to the restaurant in the afternoon  right and then when it came time for  college though you played field hockey  but you also tell me was what was the  dual major environmental biology zoo all  and Fisheries and Wildlife which does  not feel like cooking it actually ties  in really well we work so closely with a  lot of local farmers it's nice to be  able to talk to fishermen about whether  they're using longline you know what's  going on with things like that what  brought you to New York eventually I got  offered a position at wd-50 on the Lower  East Side for wylie dufresne so I moved  out here in 2010 to work for him which  was really an enlightening and  eye-opening experience and from there  you went to Blue Hill at Stone Barns and  that was had to be really different it  was different being on the farm being at  that you know having a menu that isn't  even written being able to kind of  experiment and use this amazing produce  and ingredients that's kind of what  Kingsley has become you know we can only  do so much as chefs it really is based  on how good your ingredients are when  you start there was a great quote that I  read from you some girls plan their  weddings I plan my restaurant yeah I  mean but is this the sustainability that  you have at Kingsley is that what you  had in mind back then yeah always I mean  I you know even with the majors I was  always interested in the environment and  what goes on with that so I'm extremely  proud to say that Kingsley is a zero  landfill restaurant we produce a zero  trash everything is composted recycled a  whole animal utilization it really all  does tie in together and it's it's  become all of my interests kind of  smushed into one well it is the dish so  time for you to sign the dish and as  you're doing so we'll ask you if you  could have a meal with anyone past or  present who would it be I would have to  say Ruth Bader Ginsburg oh I think  that's our first vote first you know  yeah oh I love I love me some RBG I'm  sure she'd like to Roxanne's Bern thank  you thank you so much and for more on  Chef Spruance  and the dish you can head to our website  at CBS this morning calm
 

